>
  <p>Percentages of your one-rep max are one of the most misunderstood tools in a program. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel clears it up.</p>

  <h2>Percentage is intensity, not effort</h2>
  <p>Training percentage is lifting intensity. It is not aggression or effort, which people often assume. The percentage of your one-rep max gives you a rep range you should be able to do.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Example: a 325 max, 70% is right around 250 pounds, and someone could generally do 5 to 8 reps there.</JordanQuote>

  <Media type="image" alt="Table converting one-rep max percentages into working weights for a 325 lb max" caption="A 325 lb max converted to working weights." brief="Graphic: percentage chart for a 325 lb max (70% about 250 lb)." />

  <h2>Strength vs. size ranges</h2>
  <p>Jordan uses mostly the same percentages for both. In strength work you're not trying to get to failure. In hypertrophy you're going for mechanical tension and getting close to failure. See <a href="/rep-ranges-for-strength-and-muscle/">rep ranges for strength and muscle</a>.</p>

  <h2>When to test a true max</h2>
  <p>Jordan tests true maxes for clients with a specific goal to train their one-rep max and hit a particular number. Otherwise he uses a 3-rep or 5-rep max as a proxy for whether they've gotten stronger lift to lift. He stays right around 3 to 5 reps; above that the numbers aren't very reliable.</p>

  <h2>How to know you're getting stronger</h2>
  <p>If the weights you're moving continually go up according to the schedule and progression you should be following, every single week, you're getting stronger.</p>
